@page "/docs/get-started/blazor-web-app"

@* Setting up a new Blazor Web App. Each step was checked against `dotnet new blazor --empty` on
   .NET 10: the app builds, serves the stylesheet and posts the first page's form. *@

<ArticlePage Section="get-started" Slug="blazor-web-app">
    <Lead>
        A new Blazor Web App needs one package, a stylesheet, the theme and two lines in <Code>Program.cs</Code>. Pages render
        on the server as static HTML by default, and you make interactive only the ones that need it.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Create the app</Heading>
        <TextBlock>You need the .NET 10 SDK. Start from the empty template, which has no Bootstrap or sample styles to override IzzyBlazor's:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">dotnet new blazor --empty -o MyApp</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Its pages are static by default, and any page can be made interactive on the server. Add <Code>--interactivity None</Code>
            for an app that never opens a circuit. For WebAssembly or Auto, see
            <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/webassembly-and-auto">WebAssembly or Auto</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Install the package</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="bash">dotnet add package IzzyBlazor --prerelease</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Add the stylesheet and the theme</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Link the stylesheet before your own, and put <Code>IzzyTheme</Code> in the <Code>head</Code>:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/App.razor" HighlightLines="6,9">@AppHead</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/izzy-theme">IzzyTheme</Anchor> sets the preset, brand color and fonts, as in
            <Code>&lt;IzzyTheme Preset="ThemePreset.Compact" Brand="#0a7d5a" /&gt;</Code>. It renders on the server, so the
            theme is there on first paint with no script. There's no script tag to add either: Blazor loads IzzyBlazor's
            script by itself.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Import the components</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Add one line to your imports:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/_Imports.razor">@@using IzzyBlazor</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Prefer this to a <Code>global using</Code>: it brings the components into <Code>.razor</Code> files only, so names
            such as <Code>Table</Code> and <Code>Column</Code> don't meet EF Core's attributes in your entity classes.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Register the services</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Add the services, and map IzzyBlazor's endpoints after your components:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs" HighlightLines="2,10">@ProgramCs</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>AddIzzyBlazor</Code> adds the toast and dialog services, carries toasts over a redirect, and lets components
            read their cookies on the server, such as the color scheme someone picked. <Code>MapIzzyBlazor</Code> adds the
            endpoint <Anchor Href="docs/components/color-scheme-switch">ColorSchemeSwitch</Anchor> uses with JavaScript off.
            Other components need neither.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Add toasts and dialogs</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            If you show toasts, put <Anchor Href="docs/components/toast-region">ToastRegion</Anchor> in your layout, once. It
            works on static pages too:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/Layout/MainLayout.razor" HighlightLines="5">@Layout</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            To open dialogs from C# with <Code>DialogService</Code>, put one <Code>&lt;DialogHost /&gt;</Code> on the interactive
            page that opens them, or in the layout with a render mode on the host:
            <Code>&lt;DialogHost @@rendermode="InteractiveServer" /&gt;</Code>. Dialogs written in the page need neither; see
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/dialog">Dialog</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Build a first page</Heading>
        <TextBlock>A form is a good first page, because it shows what static rendering does on its own:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/Pages/Signup.razor">@Signup</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Run the app and open <Code>/signup</Code>. The form posts, validates and shows its message with no circuit, and with
            JavaScript turned off too. Add <Code>@@rendermode InteractiveServer</Code> under <Code>@@page</Code> and the same
            markup runs over a circuit instead. See <Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor> for the rest.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">From the full template</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Started without <Code>--empty</Code>? Two things in the sample app get in the way:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Its styles win over IzzyBlazor's.</strong> IzzyBlazor's CSS sits in cascade layers so your own CSS
                always wins, and that includes Bootstrap and the template's <Code>app.css</Code>: button links turn blue on blue
                and headings change size. Remove the Bootstrap link and the template's rules in <Code>app.css</Code>, or
                <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/existing-app#keep-bootstrap">put Bootstrap in a layer</Anchor>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Its <Code>NavMenu</Code> clashes with IzzyBlazor's.</strong> The build fails with
                <Code>RZ9985: Multiple components use the tag 'NavMenu'</Code>. Rename the template's component, or write its
                full name in <Code>MainLayout.razor</Code>: <Code>&lt;MyApp.Components.Layout.NavMenu /&gt;</Code>.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/app-shell">AppShell</Anchor>: a header, sidebar and main area for your layout.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components">Components</Anchor>: every component, with what it does in each mode.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/style-props">Style props</Anchor>: spacing, sizes and colors on any component.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string AppHead = """
        <head>
            <meta charset="utf-8" />
            <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
            <base href="/" />
            <ResourcePreloader />
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/IzzyBlazor.Core/izzyblazor.css"]" />
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["MyApp.styles.css"]" />
            <IzzyTheme />
            <ImportMap />
            <HeadOutlet />
        </head>
        """;

    private const string ProgramCs = """
        var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
        builder.Services.AddIzzyBlazor();
        builder.Services.AddRazorComponents()
            .AddInteractiveServerComponents();

        var app = builder.Build();
        // ...
        app.MapRazorComponents<App>()
            .AddInteractiveServerRenderMode();
        app.MapIzzyBlazor();

        app.Run();
        """;

    private const string Layout = """
        @inherits LayoutComponentBase

        @Body

        <ToastRegion />
        """;

    private const string Signup = """
        @page "/signup"
        @using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations

        <Container Size="ContainerSize.Sm" PaddingY="8">
            <Form Model="Model" FormName="signup" OnValidSubmit="Save">
                <TextField @bind-Value="Model!.Email" AutoComplete="email" />
                <Button Submit>Sign up</Button>
            </Form>
            @if (_signedUp)
            {
                <Alert Status="Status.Success">Thanks! We'll write to @Model!.Email.</Alert>
            }
        </Container>

        @code {
            [SupplyParameterFromForm(FormName = "signup")]
            private SignupModel? Model { get; set; }

            private bool _signedUp;

            protected override void OnInitialized() => Model ??= new();

            private void Save() => _signedUp = true;

            public sealed class SignupModel
            {
                [Required, EmailAddress]
                public string? Email { get; set; }
            }
        }
        """;
}
